{"content":"q28.\n\n**Runners the server actually starts** (all via `Server.Start` → `startBackgroundRunners`, `server/server.go:129,153`):\n\n- **s3presign runner (one-shot warm-up)** — `s3presignRunner.RunOnce(ctx)` called synchronously during `startBackgroundRunners` (`server/server.go:162-163`); trigger = server startup, once.\n- **s3presign runner (continuous loop)** — `s3presign.Runner.Run` in its own goroutine with a dedicated cancellable context (`server/server.go:156-171`); trigger = `time.NewTicker(runnerInterval)` where `runnerInterval = 12h` (`server/runner/s3presign/runner.go:27,29-41`). Tracked in `backgroundRunnerWG`, stopped by `s3Cancel` on `Shutdown` (`server/server.go:140,176-182`).\n- **HTTP serve goroutine** — `s.httpServer.Serve(listener)` (`server/server.go:123-128`); trigger = incoming connections, not a schedule. Not registered in the runner WG; shut down via `shutdownHTTPServer`.\n\n**Other long-lived goroutines started indirectly** (not part of `backgroundRunnerCancels`/WG, so easy to miss):\n\n- **store cache cleanup loops (×3)** — `go c.cleanupLoop()` in `cache.New` (`store/cache/cache.go:83`), one each for `instanceSettingCache`, `userCache`, `userSettingCache` (`store/store.go:51-53`); trigger = `time.NewTicker(CleanupInterval)` = 5 min (`store/store.go:42`, `store/cache/cache.go:200-215`).\n- **async webhook workers (×4)** — package `init()` spawns 4 goroutines draining `asyncPostQueue` (`internal/webhook/webhook.go:39-54`); trigger = queue send, event-driven, not scheduled.\n- **async email workers (×2)** — package `init()` draining `asyncEmailQueue` (`internal/email/email.go:14-30`); trigger = queue send, event-driven.\n- **SSE heartbeat ticker** — per-request, inside the SSE handler (`server/router/api/v1/sse_handler.go:64`), lives for the connection's lifetime; trigger = `sseHeartbeatInterval` ticker. Connections are force-closed by `sseHub.Close()` on shutdown (`server/server.go:203-208`).\n\n**Unused scheduling / runner infrastructure:**\n\n- **`internal/scheduler` (cron scheduler) is entirely unused by the server.** It's a complete implementation — cron parser, job registry, `Start`/`Stop`, middleware (`internal/scheduler/scheduler.go`, `parser.go`, `job.go`, `middleware.go`, documented in `internal/scheduler/doc.go`) — but the only importers are its own tests (`internal/scheduler/example_test.go:10`, `internal/scheduler/integration_test.go:13`). The code graph reports a certified zero for callers of `scheduler.New` and blast radius confined to `internal/scheduler/scheduler.go`. No production wiring; the one real periodic job (s3presign) hand-rolls a ticker instead.\n- **`server/runner/memopayload.Runner` is never run as a background runner.** `memopayload.NewRunner` and `Runner.RunOnce` have zero callers (certified zero in the graph); only the free function `memopayload.RebuildMemoPayload` is used, called inline on memo create/update in `server/router/api/v1/memo_service.go:108,447`. So the batch payload-rebuild runner is dead scheduling capability — its trigger would have to be a migration/backfill that no longer invokes it.","iteration":16,"turn":1,"type":"assistant"}
